that? It's a noisy camera. Still noisy after the technical adaptation? Well the technical adaptation

192
00:19:01,839 --> 00:19:08,720
was for the quiet scenes, and so that box that would go around the camera was so heavy, we couldn't

193
00:19:08,720 --> 00:19:14,799
take it on the boat, so the siren song I was hearing was the IMAX camera. But you made it that look

194
00:19:14,799 --> 00:19:22,240
very real. We thought you were being tormented by the siren. Chris very wisely, the morning we shot

195
00:19:22,240 --> 00:19:27,839
that he was waiting for me on the dock before we left. Normally he would go ahead with the camera

196
00:19:27,839 --> 00:19:32,879
boat so that they would be ready on the boat when we got there, and on that morning he was waiting

197
00:19:32,879 --> 00:19:38,960
for me. He said, Hojta and I were talking and we think we should start with you, and that's the

198
00:19:38,960 --> 00:19:43,919
emotional backbone of this scene, and I want to shoot till we're happy, and then we'll get the

199
00:19:44,000 --> 00:19:50,160
other pieces. And what he did in doing that was, I knew I had an hour on this boat ride that I could

200
00:19:50,160 --> 00:19:56,160
sequester myself from everybody else and go get my mind right so that I could step off the boat

201
00:19:56,160 --> 00:20:02,480
and go right into work, and it was a real gift. He understands the actors process well enough to know

202
00:20:02,480 --> 00:20:06,800
that I'd be thinking about, well when is this scene coming up, and trying to manage my energy for

203
00:20:06,800 --> 00:20:12,480
the whole day, and what he did was he basically gave me a curtain time like they do in the theater.

204
00:20:12,480 --> 00:20:19,599
At eight o'clock the show begins, and he basically said, yeah in one hour we start with you, and he

205
00:20:19,599 --> 00:20:25,440
also said we shoot till we're happy, which was a way of saying, don't worry, we're gonna shoot,

206
00:20:26,079 --> 00:20:30,640
you're gonna have plenty of attempts at this. Yeah, so that you're not worried about being

207
00:20:31,360 --> 00:20:38,160
in top form in one or two takes. So much of the job is about relaxation, and when you hold on too

208
00:20:38,160 --> 00:20:44,080
tightly you can actually do yourself a disservice. So he gave me the permission to relax and know that

209
00:20:44,080 --> 00:20:49,840
I would have plenty of time. One of the big early scenes when the Penelope slapped you,

210
00:20:50,720 --> 00:20:57,040
right, where you told her to remarry, to marry someone else in time. Was that design, was that

211
00:20:57,040 --> 00:21:02,560
meant to be, or did she? Yeah, it was in the script. But it looked very real, very spontaneous.

212
00:21:02,559 --> 00:21:08,079
Yeah, well Annie is so wonderful, because the slap is spontaneous for Penelope. She doesn't know

213
00:21:08,079 --> 00:21:13,919
she's gonna hit him until he says it, and it just comes out of her, and he doesn't even blame her

214
00:21:13,919 --> 00:21:18,079
for it, you know. He understands why she's, because what he's asking is very serious, and

215
00:21:19,200 --> 00:21:25,119
but I love that exchange between them, but it was always in the script. Chris always intended for

216
00:21:25,119 --> 00:21:29,359
them. But how do you make it so real? Because sometimes- That's Annie, she's amazing. How about

217
00:21:29,359 --> 00:21:35,519
you? That your reaction was also very authentic. Look, you know, we couldn't tell at all that it was

218
00:21:35,519 --> 00:21:40,240
spontaneous, something that just came up naturally on the scene. Chris and I talked about it after

219
00:21:40,240 --> 00:21:46,799
it happened the first time, and because Odysseus, it's not a painful slap. It wouldn't be an issue

220
00:21:46,799 --> 00:21:54,079
of pain. He's more concerned about his wife. But I'm struck since yesterday, how young you look now.

221
00:21:54,799 --> 00:22:00,000
But on some of the scenes, you look so weathered and old, you know. How is that possible? And

222
00:22:00,799 --> 00:22:04,559
was it making the film in such a hard way? Was that what it did to you?

223
00:22:05,119 --> 00:22:10,159
Well, in some ways, yeah. All of that weather and all of that salt water and all of that is, we all were

224
00:22:10,159 --> 00:22:16,879
pretty weather-beaten on the movie. But then there are three distinct phases in Odysseus' life that

225
00:22:16,879 --> 00:22:22,559
are covered, and he's a young man, he's a middle-aged man, he's my age, and then he's older.

226
00:22:23,359 --> 00:22:27,679
And so for the older things, they have, you know, techniques with, you know,

227
00:22:28,240 --> 00:22:32,240
stipple where they're in aging, where they'll stretch the skin and put glue and then release it

228
00:22:32,240 --> 00:22:36,559
and give you more wrinkles and things like that. Yeah, it's a very, very wonderful makeup artist.

229
00:22:36,559 --> 00:22:42,240
When I went to do press and I hadn't seen John Leguizamo, who plays Umeas in the film, I hadn't

230
00:22:42,240 --> 00:22:45,759
seen him for a long time, and I walked into the room and the first thing I said was, you're so young,

231
00:22:46,799 --> 00:22:51,279
because I'd been used to seeing him with white hair. The magic of film. Magic of film.

232
00:22:51,279 --> 00:22:58,799
You had so much tough treatment in the film and everything that a man could suffer, you pretty

233
00:22:58,799 --> 00:23:04,240
much suffered. Was there a moment when you're like, you really have to do some psychological work so

234
00:23:04,240 --> 00:23:09,119
that, because it's at the end of your limit, was there ever a moment like that? I would think

235
00:23:09,119 --> 00:23:14,960
about it every morning. I would really want to just set my intention for the day and then stretch a

236
00:23:14,960 --> 00:23:23,360
little bit to get my body ready and then go to work. But the fact was, everything that was happening

237
00:23:23,360 --> 00:23:29,120
to me on screen was happening to everybody else off screen. And so I'm looking at my friends who

238
00:23:29,120 --> 00:23:35,279
are trying to do this very ambitious movie. We're trying to fulfill Chris's vision and we were all

239
00:23:35,279 --> 00:23:40,240
out there together. There was a lot of comfort in that, in feeling that the group was going through

240
00:23:40,240 --> 00:23:46,559
it together. And Chris, if we were ever cold and wet, you could just look and, it's not like Chris

241
00:23:46,559 --> 00:23:51,200
had a special place to go. He was just as cold and just as wet. And there was a pride, I think,

242
00:23:51,200 --> 00:23:56,559
for all of us, in not complaining ever. Chris gets the finest craftsmen in the world at what they do.

243
00:23:56,559 --> 00:24:01,680
And then he asks everybody to push the envelope. That's the thing that's exciting about these

244
00:24:01,680 --> 00:24:10,480
pictures is you can feel everybody straining, straining to do better than they've done before,

245
00:24:10,480 --> 00:24:16,960
to just fulfill what's being asked of them. And you certainly showed that leadership on the screen.

246
00:24:16,960 --> 00:24:21,600
You were like the Ancon said, what do you make of the assessment? He said, you have to be in

247
00:24:21,600 --> 00:24:27,680
certain time in your life and in your career to be able to do this role. How do you understand that?

248
00:24:28,240 --> 00:24:34,080
I think what the character is grappling with internally, I would not have understood. I would

249
00:24:34,080 --> 00:24:39,200
have understood it intellectually, but to express it and to feel it and to live it and to express it.

250
00:24:39,200 --> 00:24:46,080
What is it that the character was grappling with? Odysseus is a character who has made many mistakes

251
00:24:46,080 --> 00:24:50,799
and he's living with the consequences of those decisions that he's made. And I think once you

252
00:24:50,799 --> 00:24:56,400
get to middle age, no matter who you are, you'll have reflected on those. You'll be carrying those.

253
00:24:56,400 --> 00:24:59,920
And the character needed to look like he was carrying something.

254
00:25:00,960 --> 00:25:06,160
Maybe does that explain the scene where he was just kind of lying on the raft and let the waves

255
00:25:06,160 --> 00:25:10,960
take him? What does that scene say in the sequence of what you were just talking about?

256
00:25:10,960 --> 00:25:13,440
It's the one thing that he's never done, which is...

257
00:25:13,440 --> 00:25:14,940
He always fought.

258
00:25:14,240 --> 00:25:19,360
Yeah, he always fought his fate and he has to give himself up. He has to surrender.

259
00:25:19,360 --> 00:25:22,720
And he can't go home until he surrenders.

260
00:25:23,360 --> 00:25:28,640
But what's the moral in that? That we should surrender or that we should let life carry us?

261
00:25:28,640 --> 00:25:33,519
I think it's that we can control the things that we can control, but we have to be at peace with

262
00:25:33,519 --> 00:25:39,440
what we can't control. And we'll be in a battle with ourselves until we can find acceptance

263
00:25:39,440 --> 00:25:41,440
in the places where we need to find acceptance.

264
00:25:42,160 --> 00:25:46,640
Well, maybe because he can do that because he fought. He had fought the hardest.

265
00:25:46,640 --> 00:25:51,279
Do you remember the time, the moment when he said, I don't really want to go to home?

266
00:25:51,359 --> 00:25:55,039
That line kind of stayed with some people. This is a film about homecoming.

267
00:25:55,039 --> 00:25:56,559
What do you mean you didn't want to go home?

268
00:25:56,559 --> 00:26:02,559
It's one of the, to me, the great ideas in Chris's adaptation. Historically, we thought of it as

269
00:26:02,559 --> 00:26:07,200
he's doing everything he can to get home. But is he, right?

270
00:26:07,200 --> 00:26:08,700
There is a but.

271
00:26:08,240 --> 00:26:11,440
That's up for the audience to decide, oh, what would that mean?

272
00:26:11,440 --> 00:26:17,599
How could it mean otherwise? I mean, isn't homecoming in western eastern culture, in Chinese culture,

273
00:26:17,599 --> 00:26:22,559
especially like the most important thing, you know, almost in life?

274
00:26:22,559 --> 00:26:26,000
Without question. And this is the foundational homecoming story.

275
00:26:26,000 --> 00:26:29,759
But I thought it was a very interesting idea.

276
00:26:30,639 --> 00:26:37,359
In fact, the first thing he's going to need to do when he goes home is to face his wife

277
00:26:37,359 --> 00:26:43,439
and to be honest about what he's done. And so until he can accept what he's done,

278
00:26:43,680 --> 00:26:49,200
there's something keeping him. Because, of course, the Siren song tells him that he doesn't want to go home.

279
00:26:50,480 --> 00:26:54,320
And that's the first time that he hears that. It's a radical idea.

280
00:26:55,360 --> 00:27:01,680
What kind of roles do you want to explore going forward, having played Odysseus?

281
00:27:01,680 --> 00:27:06,880
I don't know if I'll ever see a role as wonderful as this one. It's a really spectacular.

282
00:27:07,519 --> 00:27:12,480
Spectacular. Would you call the defining role of your life or some other word?

283
00:27:12,720 --> 00:27:19,519
In western literature, it's such an iconic role. And the chance to play that was really

284
00:27:20,079 --> 00:27:25,519
privileged. And going forward, I really will keep choosing projects based on the director.

285
00:27:26,720 --> 00:27:29,680
For me, at this point, I've read so many thousands and thousands of screenplays,

286
00:27:29,680 --> 00:27:35,599
and I've written so many screenplays that something gives me a feeling when I read something.

287
00:27:35,599 --> 00:27:38,960
I'm less analytical and more intuitive about it.

288
00:27:38,960 --> 00:27:41,839
Will your intuition ever bring you to China again?

289
00:27:41,839 --> 00:27:47,599
I hope so. I really hope so. We had an amazing experience here. We lived here for half a year.

290
00:27:48,159 --> 00:27:55,279
We were in Beijing for a lot of it. We were in Qingdao. At the end, we were in the desert with

291
00:27:55,279 --> 00:28:00,639
the painted mountains. It was such an extraordinary time in our lives and such an extraordinary

292
00:28:00,639 --> 00:28:04,319
experience for my whole family. So I would love to come back.

293
00:28:05,279 --> 00:28:07,839
We'll be welcoming you.

294
00:28:07,839 --> 00:28:09,339
Oh, good. Thank you.

295
00:28:08,960 --> 00:28:14,559
Once again, congratulations to a wonderful piece of work. And it's been a pleasure to talk to you.

296
00:28:14,559 --> 00:28:16,059
My pleasure too. Thanks.

297
00:28:15,680 --> 00:28:17,180
Thank you.

298
00:28:20,559 --> 00:28:22,059
After years of war,

299
00:28:25,360 --> 00:28:29,200
no one could stand between my men and home.

300
